<div mt-section-origin="Bookshelves/General_Chemistry/Chem1_(Lower)/13:_Acid-Base_Equilibria/13.04:_Conjugate_Pairs_and_Buffers" class="mt-section" id="section_16"><span id="What's_all_this_good_for.3F"></span><h3 class="lt-chem-45385 editable">What's all this good for?</h3>

<p class="lt-chem-45385">Suppose you have a solution of NaA whose pH is 8. Estimate the concentration of the acid HA in the solution. All you need do is look at the plot. The [HA] line crosses the line marking this pH at &ndash; log C = 6, or [HA] = 10<sup>&ndash;6</sup>. This quantity too small to significantly reduce [A<sup>&ndash;</sup>], which remains at approximately CT = 10<sup>&ndash;6</sup>&thinsp;<i>M</i>.</p>

</div></div><div mt-section-origin="Bookshelves/General_Chemistry/Chem1_(Lower)/13:_Acid-Base_Equilibria/13.04:_Conjugate_Pairs_and_Buffers" class="mt-section" id="section_17"><span id="References"></span><h2 class="lt-chem-45385 editable">References</h2>

